From: Code and Solder <codeandsolder@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom: stop live firmware after host-reported crash

The rproc core sets CRASHED for both physical fatal interrupts and
host-reported crashes. Use the actual firmware running flag to decide
whether a graceful SMP2P stop is needed. Based on Bjorn Andersson's
upstream fix for this failure mode.

Link: https://git.zx2c4.com/linux-rng/commit/?id=0ea50486978f109e6d4c32267fab01ed654a2160

Signed-off-by: Code and Solder <codeandsolder@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -210,16 +210,17 @@
  *
  * Return: 0 on success, negative errno on failure
  */
 int qcom_q6v5_request_stop(struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5, struct qcom_sysmon *sysmon)
 {
+	bool was_running = q6v5->running;
 	int ret;
 
 	q6v5->running = false;
 
-	/* Don't perform SMP2P dance if remote isn't running */
-	if (q6v5->rproc->state != RPROC_RUNNING || qcom_sysmon_shutdown_acked(sysmon))
+	/* A host-reported crash needs a stop if firmware is still running. */
+	if (!was_running || qcom_sysmon_shutdown_acked(sysmon))
 		return 0;
 
 	qcom_smem_state_update_bits(q6v5->state,
 				    BIT(q6v5->stop_bit), BIT(q6v5->stop_bit));
 
